    "A": "Research a sparse-obstacle combinatorial algorithm: deduplicate blocked coordinates, sort them, and compute each obstacle’s paths from the start minus contributions through earlier obstacles that dominate it; apply the same subtraction to the destination. Precompute factorials/inverse factorials modulo 1,000,000,007 (2N−2 is within range) and use 64-bit modular products. Handle blocked start/destination explicitly. Measure its cost across obstacle counts: the O(M²) method is not guaranteed practical by the stated bound, so do not assume sparsity alone makes it faster.",
